        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Kettlebells</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these kettlebells. They were chosen from manufacturer product pages,
            Amazon listings, warranty pages, recall records and independent coverage, and the claims on
            this page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the four mechanisms on sale — a dial that selects plates while the bell sits
            in a base, a push-and-twist lock, a magnetic pin, and plates bolted inside a shell — and
            looked for a kettlebell of each type that was listed and in stock on Amazon US and on the
            maker's own store on 19 October 2026, and that an independent source had described in
            enough detail to cross-check. Garage Gym Reviews' adjustable kettlebell roundup (updated
            August 2026) and its individual reviews of the REP, <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>BowFlex</a>, PowerBlock and Bells of Steel
            bells were the main independent sources; the Amazon listings were the second check on
            settings, prices and who sells each bell. Figures went onto the cards only where the maker
            and an independent source agreed.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The recall check</h3>
          <p>
            Every brand on this page — BowFlex, REP Fitness, PowerBlock, Bells of Steel and Titan
            Fitness — and every brand we considered and left out was searched in the CPSC's recall
            database on 19 October 2026, by name in recall titles, along with the words kettlebell,
            dumbbell and weight. We found no kettlebell recall of any brand. BowFlex has three
            strength-equipment recalls on record, none of them a kettlebell; the next section explains
            them, because the SelectTech name is shared with the recalled dumbbells. PowerBlock's one
            recall, in October 2024, covers the 5 lb grip handles sold with its Commercial Pro 100
            dumbbell set, not its kettlebells. REP Fitness's one recall record (2021) concerns strap
            safety brackets for racks, and the Titan recall alert we found (2018) concerns weightlifting
            safety straps; neither involves a kettlebell. We found nothing for Bells of Steel.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we left out, and why</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Kettlebell Kings Adjustable Competition Kettlebell.</strong> A well-known
              design that adjusts from 26 to 70 lb, but its Amazon listing showed "currently
              unavailable" on 19 October, and Garage Gym Reviews notes the brand no longer sells
              direct from its own site. We will reconsider it when it is back in stock.</li>
            <li><strong>NÜOBELL.</strong> SMRTFT, which sells NÜOBELL in the US, listed only its 80 and
              100 lb adjustable dumbbells and their stands on 19 October. We found no NÜOBELL
              adjustable kettlebell on sale.</li>
            <li><strong>Ironmaster Quick-Lock Adjustable Kettlebell.</strong> A cast-iron handle that
              takes Ironmaster's dumbbell plates and locks them with the same screw as its dumbbells.
              It is excellent value if you already own Ironmaster's system, but it is sold mainly
              direct and only works with Ironmaster plates, so it is not a stand-alone buy. Garage Gym
              Reviews describes empty handles of 12.5 or 22.5 lb and a maximum of about 80 lb with
              expansion kits.</li>
            <li><strong>Titan 10–40 lb Adjustable Kettlebell.</strong> A popular beginner bell that
              Garage Gym Reviews still lists, but on 19 October Titan's store showed it only as
              unavailable scratch-and-dent stock, so we treated it as not currently sold.</li>
            <li><strong>Plate-loaded kettlebell handles</strong> such as Titan's Plate Loadable
              Kettlebell Swing, which takes ordinary barbell plates on a loading pin. They are cheap
              and go very heavy, but they are a different tool — the plates sit below a handle rather
              than inside a bell — and most sell for well under $100, below the floor for this list.
              They are covered in the mechanisms section below.</li>
            <li><strong>Sub-$100 bells.</strong> PowerBlock's Sport 10–20 lb bell ($99) and a long tail
              of unbranded Amazon bells are cheaper. We kept the list to bells around $150 and up, where
              the build differences are clearer, and say more about cheap dial bells under <a href="#rattle-and-safety" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">rattle and safety</a>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="bowflex-recall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is the BowFlex SelectTech 840 Kettlebell Recalled?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>No.</strong> On 19 October 2026 we found no CPSC recall of the BowFlex SelectTech
            840 adjustable kettlebell. The question comes up because the kettlebell shares the
            SelectTech name and dial idea with BowFlex dumbbells that were recalled.
          </p>
          <p>
            Here is what the BowFlex recall records actually cover:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>June 2025 — SelectTech 552 and 1090 dumbbells.</strong> Johnson Health Tech
              Trading, which now owns BowFlex, recalled the Model 552 (5 to 52.5 lb) and Model 1090
              (10 to 90 lb) adjustable dumbbells, including about 3.7 million sold by Nautilus,
              because the <a href="/best-weight-plates/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>weight plates</a> can dislodge from the handle during use. The notice names
              those two dumbbell models only.</li>
            <li><strong>February 2021 — SelectTech 2080 barbell with curl bar.</strong> A dial-selected
              barbell and curl bar, recalled for an impact injury hazard.</li>
            <li><strong>June 2012 — SelectTech 1090 dumbbells.</strong> An earlier recall of 1090
              dumbbells with a specific range of date codes in the serial number.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of these notices mentions the 840 kettlebell. That is a statement about the recall
            records, not a test of the product: the 840 uses the same broad idea as the recalled
            dumbbells — a dial that selects which plates stay with the handle while it sits in a base —
            so the same habit applies. Lift the bell an inch out of its base, check that the plate
            stack you selected has come with it, and only then swing. If you buy one, register it with
            BowFlex using the serial number, because makers contact registered owners first if anything
            changes.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you already own BowFlex dumbbells, or are looking at a used pair, our <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/#check-recall" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to checking adjustable dumbbells against the recalls</a> explains where the model and serial numbers are printed and what the notices tell owners
            to do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mechanisms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Adjustable Kettlebells Work</h2>
<p>
            Every adjustable kettlebell on sale uses one of four mechanisms. The mechanism decides
            change speed, shape, durability and noise far more than the brand does.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Dial in a base (BowFlex SelectTech 840)</h3>
          <p>
            The kettlebell sits in a moulded base holding a stack of plates; turning the dial on top
            decides how many plates lock into the shell when you lift it. It is the fastest design —
            Garage Gym Reviews calls the 840 the quickest-adjusting bell it has tried — and the most
            forgiving for beginners. The costs are a plastic shell, a shape that is taller and bulkier
            than a standard bell at light settings, larger jumps between settings, and some rattle from
            the plates. <strong>Best for</strong> circuits, conditioning and general fitness at moderate
            weights, where you change weight often.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Push-and-twist (REP Fitness)</h3>
          <p>
            Plates nest inside a cast-iron shell and a plastic adjustment mechanism, pushed down and
            twisted, decides how many are locked in. The shape stays the same at every setting, which
            is why reviewers say the REP bell feels like a fixed one. Each bell covers five settings, so
            the range per bell is narrow. <strong>Best for</strong> people who want true kettlebell feel
            for swings, cleans and presses and will buy a second bell later if they outgrow the first.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Magnetic pin (PowerBlock)</h3>
          <p>
            The PowerBlock idea applied to a kettlebell: steel plates inside a steel shell, and a
            magnetic pin pushed in at the right slot selects the weight. It is quick, has few moving
            parts and keeps one handle diameter at every weight. The shape is squarer, closer to a
            cowbell, which some lifters like for presses and racked positions and others dislike for
            snatches. <strong>Best for</strong> small spaces and for people who want a heavier quick-change
            bell than a dial design offers.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Plates bolted inside a shell (Bells of Steel, Titan)</h3>
          <p>
            A competition-size shell has a cavity in the base; you undo a screw with the supplied
            key and wrench, add or remove iron plates, and bolt it back up. Nothing can disengage on
            its own, the shell keeps competition dimensions at every weight, and the build is all
            metal. The cost is time: Bells of Steel itself quotes one to two minutes per change and says
            the design is not for fast transitions. <strong>Best for</strong> people who train at one
            weight per session, want small steps for progression, or train kettlebell sport.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Plate-loaded handles</h3>
          <p>
            Not really a kettlebell: a handle above a loading pin that takes standard or <a href="/best-barbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Olympic barbell</a> plates, held by a collar. It is the cheapest way to go very heavy if you already own plates,
            and it suits swings and deadlift variations. The plates sit low and wide, so cleans,
            snatches and presses feel nothing like a real bell, and a loose collar is a hazard. <strong>Best for</strong> heavy swings on a budget when you already own a barbell set — see
            our <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight bench guide</a> and the rest of the Home Gym series if you are building that kind of setup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Weight Range and Increments: What You Actually Need</h2>
<p>
            Fixed kettlebells traditionally jump 4 kg at a time — 8, 12, 16, 20, 24 kg — and most
            people own two or three. Adjustable bells promise to fill the gaps, but the range and the
            step size vary enormously, so match them to how you train.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Swings and deadlifts</strong> use the heaviest weight, because the hips drive
              the bell. Many people outgrow a 16 kg (35 lb) bell for two-handed swings fairly quickly.
              If swings are the core of your training, the REP 16–24 kg bell, the PowerBlock 35–62 lb
              bell, or a 32 kg Titan or Bells of Steel bell will last longer than a light-range
              bell.</li>
            <li><strong>Presses, Turkish get-ups and snatches</strong> use much lighter weights, and the
              jump between settings matters most here. Going from 12 kg to 16 kg on an overhead press is
              a one-third increase. The Bells of Steel bell's 0.5 kg steps and REP's 2 kg steps make
              that progression gentler; BowFlex's jumps of up to 10 lb (from 25 to 35 lb) are the
              coarsest on this page.</li>
            <li><strong>Goblet squats, rows and lunges</strong> sit in between and are well served by
              almost any bell here.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A useful rule: buy for your heaviest swing, then check that the lightest setting is light
            enough for your presses. If no single bell covers both, two adjustable bells — or one
            adjustable and one fixed — usually make more sense than a single bell with a huge range and
            slow changes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Remember that maker ranges are what the bell can be set to, not what you should lift. If you
            are new to kettlebells, spend the first weeks learning the hinge and the swing with a light
            setting, ideally under a coach's eye.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="handle-and-shape" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Handle, Shape and Competition Sizing</h2>
<p>
            Two things change how a kettlebell feels in use: the handle and the shape of the bell.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Handle diameter.</strong> A 35 mm handle is the competition standard, and Bells of
            Steel and Titan's competition shells use it at every weight. REP lists 34.6 mm on its
            kilogram bells and 35 mm on its pound bell; PowerBlock lists 32.77 mm on both of its Pro
            bells. Garage Gym Reviews describes the BowFlex 840 handle as noticeably thin and says it
            does not hold chalk well; BowFlex does not publish a diameter. Thinner handles suit smaller
            hands and high-rep work; thicker ones are easier to control on cleans and snatches once you
            are used to them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Window and shape.</strong> Competition shells keep the same outside size whatever
            they weigh, so the bell sits in the same place on your forearm in the rack position and
            travels the same path in a snatch at 12 kg as at 32 kg. Garage Gym Reviews notes that the
            narrow competition window can be tight for two-handed swings if you have large hands. The
            BowFlex shell is taller than a standard bell and bulky at light settings, and the PowerBlock
            is squarer and more compact. Both work well for swings, goblet squats and rows; the
            difference shows most in cleans, snatches and get-ups.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Base.</strong> A flat base matters if you will do push-ups or renegade rows on the
            bells, or simply want them to stand still. Titan specifically calls out its flat base for
            floor work; the dial designs must sit in their own base to be adjusted, which means the base
            lives on your floor too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rattle-and-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Rattle, Locking and Safe Use</h2>
<p>
            Rattle is the most common complaint about adjustable kettlebells. A little noise is normal;
            movement you can feel is a reason to stop and check.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Why bells rattle.</strong> Any design with plates inside a shell leaves some
              clearance so the plates can be added and removed. Dial designs need the most, because the
              plates must drop out cleanly into the base. Garage Gym Reviews notes plate rattle on the
              BowFlex 840 and some reports of the PowerBlock's pin rattling; REP says its locked plates
              have minimal shift, a claim Garage Gym Reviews' tester agreed with; Bells of Steel says
              tight internal fitment minimises shifting.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the lock every time.</strong> On dial and pin designs, lift the bell an
              inch, look, and set it down again if the stack you selected has not come with it. On
              bolted designs, check the screw is tight before the first set and after every change.</li>
            <li><strong>Do not drop, slam or throw.</strong> REP says plainly that its bell is not
              designed to be slammed or dropped and that doing so could damage it to the point it
              cannot hold the plates securely. Treat every adjustable bell the same way, whatever the
              shell is made of.</li>
            <li><strong>Clear space and a sound floor.</strong> Swings need room in front and behind,
              and a bell that slips out of tired hands travels. Train on rubber flooring, well away from
              windows, televisions, pets and children; our <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a> covers what thickness to put under free weights.</li>
            <li><strong>Mind the neighbours.</strong> Setting a kettlebell down hard is loud on a
              suspended floor. If you train upstairs or in an apartment, put it down under control on a
              mat; the same thinking applies as in our guide to <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">exercising in an apartment</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Be wary of cheap dial bells.</strong> Unbranded dial kettlebells on Amazon often
              cost a third of the BowFlex. We have not carded any because we could not find independent
              detail on their locking parts, and the adjustable-dumbbell recalls of recent years all
              involved plates coming loose from dial-type mechanisms.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="adjustable-vs-fixed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Adjustable vs Fixed Kettlebells</h2>
<p>
            The case for adjustable is cost and space; the case for fixed is simplicity and toughness.
            Prices on 19 October make the trade concrete.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cost.</strong> REP's 16–24 kg adjustable bell was $219.99 on its own store and on
            Amazon. Buying the same five weights as fixed competition bells from Titan the same day — 16,
            18, 20, 22 and 24 kg at $103.97, $113.97, $126.97, $119.97 and $147.97 — comes to $612.85
            before shipping. Titan's own 12–32 kg adjustable bell was $212.97. Even allowing that most
            people would buy three fixed bells rather than five, an adjustable bell usually wins on
            cost once you want more than two weights.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Space.</strong> One bell takes the space of one bell; a row of five fixed bells needs
            a shelf or a rack, which is fine in a garage and awkward in a spare room.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speed.</strong> Fixed bells change as fast as you can put one down and pick up
            another. Dial and pin designs take seconds; bolted competition designs take a minute or two,
            which rules them out for workouts that switch weights between exercises.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Toughness.</strong> A cast-iron fixed bell can be dropped on a rubber floor for
            decades. No adjustable bell on this page is sold as drop-safe.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>So which?</strong> If you train alone, have little space and want more than two
            weights, buy adjustable. If you train with a partner, run circuits that need two weights at
            once, or drop bells at the end of hard sets, buy two or three fixed bells instead — or one
            adjustable for presses and one heavy fixed bell for swings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you mainly want cardio and conditioning</strong>, a kettlebell is a good
            complement but not a substitute for a machine or a rope. Our guides to <a href="/treadmill-hiit-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill HIIT workouts</a> and the <a href="/best-jump-ropes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best jump ropes</a> cover two cheap ways to train hard at home, and if you want jumping work, our <a href="/best-plyo-boxes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">plyo box guide</a> covers boxes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a full free-weight setup</strong>, a set of fixed dumbbells on a rack may
            serve you better than one bell; our <a href="/best-dumbbell-sets/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dumbbell sets guide</a> compares rubber and urethane sets, cost per pound and rack footprints.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If space and budget are tight</strong>, resistance bands cover a surprising amount of
            pulling, pressing and hinge work for very little money; our <a href="/best-resistance-bands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">resistance band guide</a> explains the types.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have back or shoulder trouble</strong>, ballistic kettlebell work is not where
            to start. Get advice from a qualified professional, and consider slower strength work first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already own a barbell and plates</strong>, a plate-loaded kettlebell handle is
            the cheap way to heavy swings, though it will not do cleans or snatches well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying an Adjustable Kettlebell</h2>
          <p>Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and annoying to fix afterwards.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Choosing by maximum weight alone</h3>
              <p>Two bells with a 40 lb maximum can be very different if one moves in 2 kg steps and the other jumps 10 lb. Check the full list of settings against your heaviest swing and your lightest press, not just the top number on the box.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a slow-change bell for circuit training</h3>
              <p>Bolted competition bells take a minute or two per change. That is fine for a session at one weight and miserable for a circuit that switches between swings and presses. If your workouts change weight often, buy a dial or pin design, or two bells.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping the lock check</h3>
              <p>Dial and pin designs can look locked while the bell sits in its base. Lift it an inch, look at the plate stack, and only then swing. On bolted designs, check the screw is tight after every change. It takes seconds and prevents the failure that drove recent adjustable-weight recalls.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Swinging on a bare floor in a cramped room</h3>
              <p>A kettlebell that slips from tired hands goes a long way. Swing on rubber flooring with clear space in front and behind, away from glass, screens, pets and children, and never let go of an adjustable bell deliberately.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most home gyms, buy the REP Fitness Adjustable Kettlebell</strong> in the range that suits your training — 8–16 kg for presses and lighter work, 16–24 kg for
            swings. Choose the BowFlex SelectTech 840 if fast changes matter more than feel, the
            PowerBlock Pro if you want a compact quick-change bell that goes past 50 lb, and the Bells of
            Steel or Titan competition bell if you train at one weight per session and want small steps
            or a 32 kg ceiling.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy, check the lock before every set, lower rather than drop, and give the bell a
            proper floor to live on — <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our gym flooring guide</a> covers what to put under it.
          </p>
        </div>

