        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best adjustable kettlebell?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most home gyms, the REP Fitness Adjustable Kettlebell, because it keeps a competition-style shape at every setting, changes quickly and carries a lifetime warranty. The BowFlex SelectTech 840 is faster and cheaper for lighter work, and the Bells of Steel and Titan competition bells suit people who want small steps or a 32 kg ceiling and can accept slower changes.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the BowFlex SelectTech 840 kettlebell recalled?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              We found no CPSC recall of the SelectTech 840 kettlebell on 19 October 2026. BowFlex's June 2025 recall covers only the SelectTech 552 and 1090 adjustable dumbbells, and its 2021 and 2012 recalls cover a SelectTech barbell and older 1090 dumbbells. As with any dial design, check that the plates you selected have come with the bell before each set.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are adjustable kettlebells worth it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually, if you want more than two weights and have limited space. One adjustable bell can cost less than three fixed bells. They are less worth it if you train with a partner, need two weights in the same workout, or tend to drop kettlebells, because fixed cast-iron bells are simpler, faster to switch and much tougher.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do adjustable kettlebells rattle?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some do. Plates inside a shell need a little clearance, and dial designs need the most. Reviewers note some rattle on the BowFlex 840 and occasional pin rattle on the PowerBlock, while REP and Bells of Steel say their designs minimise plate movement. Any rattle you can feel, rather than just hear, is a reason to stop and check the lock.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What weight adjustable kettlebell should I buy?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Buy for your heaviest swing, then check the lightest setting suits your presses. Many people outgrow a 16 kg (35 lb) maximum for two-handed swings, so a bell reaching 24 kg or more lasts longer. If one bell cannot cover both swings and presses, two bells, or one adjustable and one fixed, usually work better than a single bell with a huge range.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you drop an adjustable kettlebell?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No adjustable kettlebell on this page is sold as drop-safe. REP says its bell is not designed to be slammed or dropped and that doing so could damage it so it cannot hold the plates securely. Lower every adjustable bell under control, and buy fixed cast-iron bells if you know you drop weights at the end of hard sets.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

